  if (offset >= length) mcpFail(`comment ${range.from} is ${length} characters long; \`body_offset\` ${offset} is past its end.`);
}
```

Hmm, but careful: `body_offset` could be passed for a comment with no string body → length 0 → offset 0 >= 0 → fail. But offset 0 is only "past the end" if body is empty; arguably `body_offset: 0` on an empty comment should just return the empty body. Use `offset > 0 && offset >= length`? If offset is 0 and length 0, return empty. Let me define: fail when `offset > 0 && offset >= length`? If offset = length > 0, nothing to read → fail. If offset > length → fail. So `offset >= length && offset > 0`. Hmm, for offset=0,length=0 return empty. OK: `if (offset >= length && offset > 0) fail`.

Actually simpler: `if (offset > 0 && offset >= length)`. Fine.

Now the budget:

```ts
// The per-comment budget is the whole tool budget split across the comments this
// read selects, not a fixed fifth. A fifth was chosen so five comments fill the
// budget, and it made the common case -- one comment, reached from a search hit --
